===== P3/P4/P5/P-N4/P-N5/P-N6: structural-completeness
nothing about algorithms outside this family.

The theorem is argued by case analysis over seven structural blocks
(Section~\ref{sec:blocks}; proof sketches in
Section~\ref{sec:theorem}, not yet independently reviewed) under
standing assumptions: Tate's
isogeny theorem, Howe's gluing theorem, Gaudry's and Diem--Thom\'e's
complexity bounds for hyperelliptic index calculus, and the
assumption that prime-field hyperelliptic DLP is not
sub-exponentially solvable (Section~\ref{sec:theorem}).
The empirical checks are bounded: the structural conditions are
computed for the four deployed curves only
(Section~\ref{sec:empirical}); the twist $\ell$-rank lemma is
checked numerically for $\ell \in \{3,5,7,11,13\}$ on $j = 0$ curves over
$\FF_p$ with $p < 500$; the split-Jacobian toy sweep covers primes
$p \equiv 1 \pmod 6$ with $p \le 1000$, and the CM-73 sweep those with
$p \le 5000$. A surprising
The result is, predictably, a null: every direction is closed.
The value lies in the \emph{completeness} of the closure~--- the
theorem turns a folklore claim (``ECDLP is hard for prime-field
ECC'') into a rigorously decomposable, mechanically verifiable
$p \not\equiv 0 \pmod \ell$ (both scripts skip $r = 0$, which drops one
instance, $(\ell,p,b) = (7,7,5)$ with $\#E = 7$).
For $\ell \in \{3,5,7\}$
(script \texttt{exp\_zprime2\_generalized\_lemma.py}; the $\ell \in \{3,5\}$
rows also in \texttt{exp\_zprime\_l\_residue.py}) there are 24\,476
instances: 8\,734 on ordinary curves ($p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$) and 15\,742 on
supersingular ones ($p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$).  All 8\,156 instances in the
lemma's hypothesis classes $r = p \bmod \ell \notin \{0, \ell{-}1\}$ are
ordinary, and none has $\ell \mid \#E^t$.  The hypothesis classes that
contain any instance are $r = 1$ for $\ell \in \{3,5\}$ and
$r \in \{1,\ldots,5\}$ for $\ell = 7$; for $\ell = 5$ the classes $r \in \{2,3\}$ contain none, so
they are not tested.  All 16\,320 instances in the excluded class
$r = \ell{-}1$ (578 ordinary, 15\,742 supersingular) have $\ell \mid \#E^t$,
as the tightness remark predicts.
The check was extended to $\ell \in \{11,13\}$ on ordinary curves only
(1\,802 instances, script \texttt{exp\_zdoubleprime\_lemma\_extended.py}).
For $\ell = 11$ only $r = 1$ contains instances (198); for $\ell = 13$, all
11 valid residue classes $r \in \{1,\ldots,11\}$ are represented
(1\,510 instances) with $0$ failures, and $r = 12 = \ell{-}1$ yields 94
failures as predicted.  Total for $\ell \in \{3,5,7,11,13\}$: 26\,278
instances, of which 10\,536 are ordinary; the 9\,864 ordinary instances that
satisfy the lemma's hypothesis give $0$ violations.  The lemma depends on
$(\ell, p, \#E)$ only, so these are 336 and 249 distinct
$(\ell, p, \#E)$ checks respectively (for $\ell \in \{3,5,7\}$: 292 and,
in the hypothesis classes, 209).  The counts and the
\noindent\textbf{CM-73 primes confirmed finite: $\{19, 37, 79, 109\}$ up to $p \le 5000$.}
