===== L5/L-N2/L6/L-N7: conductor_gap
# Conductor-gap CM pairs: crater vs floor curves with a certified large gap

**Status: constructed, Sage-verified, measured (five ecbench sessions,
2026-10-10). Result: no implemented method sees the gap; see §5.**

Reading, by the decision rule of `PROTOCOL.md`:

- Two intervals excluded 1 in the first three sessions: ic at ℓ = 263
  and rho at ℓ = 8209, which was 1.22 [1.005, 1.46]. Each rested on four
  crater workloads, and they had opposite signs across ℓ. **The rho
  interval is unexplained.** Correction (2026-10-16, SHF-20261016-3caa6f
  L5): this bullet used to call it "noise by theorem". It is not. The
  generic-invariance theorem does not cover ecbench's rho, which hashes the
  point representation (§4). The `ell8209_q29_ic16` session put rho at
  1.04 [0.91, 1.19], with a fresh target seed (20261011) and 16 targets
  per curve, so the deviation did not reproduce there. All five sessions,
  though, used the same walk seed (`measurement.seed = 1`). Still open: a
  rho-only re-test at ℓ = 8209 under a fresh measurement seed. The A/A
  arm `rho-neg-aa` matches `rho-neg` row for row in
  `results/crater_vs_floor.md`, so it does not act as an independent
  control for this question. The ic lead was followed up first.
- The follow-up with 16 targets per curve and a fresh target seed puts
  `ic.pipeline` at 0.998 [0.970, 1.026] at ℓ = 263 and 0.96 [0.87, 1.07] at
  ℓ = 8209, with the control arm at 1 in both. **No implemented method
  shows a reproducible conductor effect**, at gaps of 2^8, 2^13 and 2^17,
  on curves that share q, N, t and the Frobenius discriminant and differ
  only in `End(E)`, pending the rho re-test at ℓ = 8209 under a fresh
  measurement seed (above). Correction (2026-10-17, sheriff case
  SHF-20261017-e4d7f6, L-N2): this line used to read "No implemented method
  sees the conductor gap", which the still-unexplained ℓ = 8209 rho
  interval does not yet allow.
- This is the honest negative the falsifiers section predicted: it extends
## 6. Falsifiers

- A BSGS-type arm with `log₂ S` excluding 0: re-sample, then check the
  harness against the control arm before calling it a harness fault. The
  theorem gives `E[S] = 1` for BSGS-type arms, not `S = 1` on every finite
  sample: `S` is a bootstrap statistic over independently drawn workloads,
  and the recorded BSGS ratios are 0.84, 0.98 and 1.25 (§5), so a 95%
  interval can exclude 1 by chance (corrected 2026-10-17, sheriff case
  SHF-20261017-e4d7f6, L-N2; earlier text said "harness fault (check the
  control arm first)"). A rho or kangaroo arm with `log₂ S` excluding 0:
  unexplained until re-tested under a fresh measurement seed. These
  methods hash the representation, so generic invariance does not rule out
The solver need not be generic: it may be structural, it may read the curve
equation, the endomorphism ring, anything. It must be a `Solver`: a
deterministic `run` that is correct on every instance (`Solver.correct`),
with a self-reported cost. Beyond that, the only things used are that `φ`
is injective and preserves `one` and `mul`. The algorithm model of
`GenericInvariance.lean` is not needed here; the two files are independent,
and together they say: methods in Nechaev's model (BSGS-type) cannot see
the conductor at all (that file), and for always-correct solvers the best
cost on the two levels differs by at most the cost of the isogeny between
them (this file).

**Scope (correction, 2026-10-17, sheriff case SHF-20261017-e4d7f6, L-N2).**
An earlier version of this comment said "generic methods cannot see the
conductor at all (that file)", unqualified; in this study's vocabulary
generic methods include `rho.*` and `kangaroo.*`, which hash the point
representation and are not instances of the first file's model. This file
bounds the best cost across levels over always-correct solvers. It says
nothing about the measured `S` of a given method, and a step-capped
randomized walk is a `Solver` only once its tape is fixed and only if it
never fails. No theorem in this file is changed.
